            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-0725",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "libcurl: Buffer Overflow in libcurl via zlib Integer Overflow"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When libcurl is asked to perform automatic gzip decompression of\ncontent-encoded HTTP responses with the `CURLOPT_ACCEPT_ENCODING` option,\n**using zlib 1.2.0.3 or older**, an attacker-controlled integer overflow would\nmake libcurl perform a buffer overflow."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-0725",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-0725\\nSeverity: LOW\\nPackage: libcurl4\\nFixed Version: \\nLink: [CVE-2025-0725](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-0725)\\nWhen libcurl is asked to perform automatic gzip decompression of\ncontent-encoded HTTP responses with the `CURLOPT_ACCEPT_ENCODING` option,\n**using zlib 1.2.0.3 or older**, an attacker-controlled integer overflow would\nmake libcurl perform a buffer overflow.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-0725**\\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n|LOW|libcurl4||[CVE-2025-0725](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-0725)|\\n\\nWhen libcurl is asked to perform automatic gzip decompression of\ncontent-encoded HTTP responses with the `CURLOPT_ACCEPT_ENCODING` option,\n**using zlib 1.2.0.3 or older**, an attacker-controlled integer overflow would\nmake libcurl perform a buffer overflow."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "2.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2025-6297",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "It was discovered that dpkg-deb does not properly sanitize directory p ..."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "It was discovered that dpkg-deb does not properly sanitize directory permissions when extracting a control member into a temporary directory, which is\ndocumented as being a safe operation even on untrusted data. This may result in leaving temporary files behind on cleanup. Given automated and repeated execution of dpkg-deb commands on\nadversarial .deb packages or with well compressible files, placed\ninside a directory with permissions not allowing removal by a non-root\nuser, this can end up in a DoS scenario due to causing disk quota\nexhaustion or disk full conditions."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-6297",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2025-6297\\nSeverity: UNKNOWN\\nPackage: dpkg\\nFixed Version: \\nLink: [CVE-2025-6297](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-6297)\\nIt was discovered that dpkg-deb does not properly sanitize directory permissions when extracting a control member into a temporary directory, which is\ndocumented as being a safe operation even on untrusted data. This may result in leaving temporary files behind on cleanup. Given automated and repeated execution of dpkg-deb commands on\nadversarial .deb packages or with well compressible files, placed\ninside a directory with permissions not allowing removal by a non-root\nuser, this can end up in a DoS scenario due to causing disk quota\nexhaustion or disk full conditions.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2025-6297**\\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n|UNKNOWN|dpkg||[CVE-2025-6297](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-6297)|\\n\\nIt was discovered that dpkg-deb does not properly sanitize directory permissions when extracting a control member into a temporary directory, which is\ndocumented as being a safe operation even on untrusted data. This may result in leaving temporary files behind on cleanup. Given automated and repeated execution of dpkg-deb commands on\nadversarial .deb packages or with well compressible files, placed\ninside a directory with permissions not allowing removal by a non-root\nuser, this can end up in a DoS scenario due to causing disk quota\nexhaustion or disk full conditions."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "UNKNOWN"
                ]
              }
